 pgf@srl02.cacs.usl.edu  (Phil G. Fraering) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-8-154:
>I need a label or word for a phenomenon that I can best describe now
>as non-fiat-currency inflation.

 I don't think you will find a single word that describes your idea,
because I don't think you have found anything more unique than an
increase in the price of X relative to Y. Until you clarify what Y is
(capital? raw materials? skilled labor?), you don't even have a coherent
idea (we can get away with letting Y default to a standard currency if we
are dealing with short enough time periods, which leads to a dangerous
habit of trying to use it for everything).

> I think the price of labor
>goes up when the number of commodities or consumer goods, or
>categories of commodities or consumer goods, increaces. It seems to me
>that labor would be much cheaper in a (assumed to be technologically
>stagnant) society where there were no consumer goods besides the one
>fiber everyone wears, the one staple food everyone eats, the minimal
>cave shelter, et cetera.

 I see no reason to expect any such correlation. Even if there is some
obstacle to buying luxury commodities, there is always room for buying
entertainment, and probably a use for labor in improving housing. I
suspect that unskilled labor was cheap relative to skilled labor due
to slavery (wages set to almost zero and hours prolonged by coercion).
